NAME
    load - read a file into memory at its load address

SYNOPSIS
    load path

DESCRIPTION
    Reads a file into memory at the load address stored in its
    directory entry, without running it, and says where:

        loaded 132 bytes at $5000

    load is a shell built-in.

    The file must fit the program area: a load address below $5000, or
    an end past $CFFF, is refused with "bad load address or size", so
    the OS ($1000..$4FFF) and the monitor are never overwritten. Other
    errors: "not found", "is a directory", "too big" (64K or more).

EXAMPLES
    load /BIN/HELLO        loaded 132 bytes at $5000
    load /FRUIT.TXT        a text file (the host tool gives $B000)

NOTES
    Every /BIN program also runs from $5000, dump and examine included,
    so they overwrite a program loaded there; to look at it, leave the
    OS (exit) and use the monitor's B or D.
    Removed from the P8X version: the .bin suffix.
    The command is the Y1/OS shell's own (os/y1os.asm; os/y1os.c); the page is
    ported from P8X os/man/load 2026-09-23.

SEE ALSO
    run, save, dump, mon
